course: dictionary
lesson: 227
title: Allergens
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An allergen is a substance that can make some people's bodies react badly. This is called an allergic reaction. Pollen, peanuts, dust mites, pet fur and bee stings are all allergens for some people, but not for everyone.
explain: Allergens are not the same for everybody. One child may sneeze at pollen, while another child feels fine. A substance is only an allergen for the people whose bodies react to it.
explain: In a skin prick test, «tiny drops of possible allergens» are put on the arm, and the skin is gently pricked. The test is a way to find out what a person might be allergic to. It does not give you an allergy.
explain: In the UK, «food businesses must tell people which of 14 major allergens are in their food». That is why food labels list allergens. Some schools are nut-free, and «allergen-free» food has none of the allergens it is being checked for.
fact: An allergen is a substance that causes an allergic reaction in some people.
fact: Common allergens include pollen, peanuts, dust mites, pet fur and bee stings.
fact: A substance is an allergen only for people whose bodies react to it.
fact: A skin prick test checks for possible allergens, and it does not give you an allergy.
fact: Food labels in the UK must show the 14 major allergens, and removing the food you can see does not make a dish allergen-free.
exercise: «In a skin prick test, tiny drops of possible allergens are put on the arm.» What is an allergen? | A kind of bee | *A substance that can cause an allergic reaction in some people | A medicine that stops itching | A type of skin test that hurts a lot
exercise: Which of these is an example of an allergen? | Sunshine | Rain | *Pollen | Snow
exercise: «Picking the egg out of a dish does not make it safe.» What does this tell us about allergens? | Allergens only live in the air | Eggs are never allergens | Picking food out always makes a dish safe | *Removing the food you can see does not remove the allergen
test: Why might one child sneeze near pet fur while another child feels fine? | Children always sneeze near animals | *Pet fur is only an allergen for the child whose body reacts to it | Pet fur is an allergen for everybody | Pet fur is never an allergen
test: Why do food labels in the UK list allergens? | To make the food taste better | To tell you how old the food is | To show the price of the food | *So people can know which major allergens are in their food
exam: What is the best meaning of allergen-free food? | *Food that does not contain the allergens it is being checked for | Food that has no flavour | Food that is only eaten by adults | Food that is very cheap
